Kubernetes Quick Diffs – Learn Fast, Deploy Smart 

Here’s a compact guide to some of the most common Kubernetes concepts — simplified:

📦 PV vs PVC
 PV (PersistentVolume): A piece of storage in the cluster.
 PVC (PersistentVolumeClaim): A request for storage by a user.

⚖️ Taint vs Annotation
 Taint: Applied to nodes to repel pods unless tolerated.
 Annotation: Metadata added to objects; not used for selection.

🎯 Label vs Annotation
 Label: Used for selection/filtering (e.g., by Deployments, Services).
 Annotation: Stores non-identifying info.

🧱 Deployment vs StatefulSet
 Deployment: For stateless apps (e.g., web servers).
 StatefulSet: For stateful apps (e.g., databases), preserves identity & order.

🧭 Service vs Ingress
 Service: Exposes a set of pods inside/outside the cluster.
 Ingress: Manages external HTTP(S) traffic with routing rules.

⚙️ ConfigMap vs Secret
 ConfigMap: Stores non-sensitive config as plain text.
 Secret: Stores sensitive data (e.g., passwords), base64-encoded.

🔁 Rolling Update vs Recreate
 Rolling Update: Gradual updates with zero downtime.
 Recreate: Deletes old pods before creating new ones.

🕵️ ReplicaSet vs ReplicationController
 ReplicaSet: Modern way to manage pod replicas.
 ReplicationController: Legacy controller, mostly deprecated.

🌐 ClusterIP vs NodePort vs LoadBalancer
 ClusterIP: Internal service access only.
 NodePort: Exposes service on a fixed port on each node.
 LoadBalancer: Provides external access via a cloud provider’s load balancer.

🔄 Stateless vs Stateful
 Stateless: No data stored between requests, easy to scale (e.g., frontend).
 Stateful: Remembers data, needs stable storage & identity (e.g., DBs).

Node Affinity vs Taint
Node Affinity: Attracts pods to specific nodes using labels.
Taint: Repels pods from nodes unless they tolerate the taint.
